---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import WebAppLink from "../../components/WebAppLink.astro";

// Issue #1106, guide 7 — rewritten for the React web client (#1258): the
// browser keeps nothing at rest. Every factual claim carries a
// `<!-- cite: ... -->` ledger entry checked by site/scripts/check-claims.mjs.
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Using the browser version"
  description="The browser version of lunarlog keeps nothing at rest — how its sign-in works, what it stores (nothing), and how it differs from a phone."
>
  <article class="doc">
    <h1 class="doc__title">Using the browser version</h1>
    <p class="doc__lede">
      lunarlog runs in a browser at
      <WebAppLink />
      — the same app, signed in to the same account, with one honest
      difference you should understand before using it on a shared
      computer: where a phone keeps your data on the device under its
      own lock, the browser keeps nothing at all — and the one
      credential it does hold (your signed-in session) sits in a cookie
      no page script can read, cleared the moment you sign out.
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md#6-security-protections; webapp/worker/cookies.ts (__Host- HttpOnly refresh cookie) -->
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>What the browser keeps</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing at rest. That is not a slogan — it is the client's build
        rule. No copy of your profiles or day entries is ever written to
        the browser's storage (no IndexedDB, no local storage, no offline
        cache), and an automated test re-asserts it on every change. What
        you see on screen is read live from your account, a window onto
        your data rather than a drawer holding a copy of it. When you
        close the tab, what was on screen is gone.
        <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md#6-security-protections; webapp/test/lint-bans.test.ts; webapp/e2e/storage-empty.spec.ts -->
      </p>
      <p>
        The sign-in itself is the narrowest a browser allows. While you
        are signed in, the working access token lives in the page's
        memory only and dies with the tab. The stay-signed-in part is a
        single HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Strict cookie — HttpOnly means
        no script running on the page, not even the app's own, can read
        it — and it is rotated every time it is used.
        <!-- cite: webapp/src/lib/auth.ts (in-memory access token); webapp/worker/cookies.ts (__Host- prefix, HttpOnly, SameSite=Strict, rotation) -->
      </p>
      <p>
        One more difference that matters: a phone keeps everything behind
        its screen lock; the browser adds no lock of its own. Anyone at
        the keyboard while your session is open can see what the window
        shows — which is why the next section is short and strict.
        <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md#6-security-protections (no browser lock of its own) -->
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Shared computers</h2>
      <p>
        On a computer other people use, the habit stays the one it always
        was: sign out when you are done, every time. Signing out clears
        the session cookie and the in-memory token — nothing else needs
        clearing, because nothing else was ever stored.
        <!-- cite: webapp/worker/auth.ts (sign-out clears the refresh cookie); webapp/src/lib/auth.ts (in-memory token dies) -->
      </p>
      <p>
        And the judgment call no guide can make for you: a cycle log for a
        teenager belongs on her own devices. A shared family computer in a
        common room, used with her knowledge, is one thing; a borrowed
        work laptop is another. When in doubt, use your phone.
        <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md#5-minors-privacy--family-profiles (minors' profiles stay with their guardians) -->
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What the browser version is good for</h2>
      <p>
        A personal computer with a password-protected account of your own:
        comfortable keyboard-and-screen editing, catching up on a
        household's profiles from a laptop, or trying lunarlog before
        installing anything. When you go back to your phone, the same
        account puts the same profiles there — every change you made in
        the browser is already in your account, because that is the only
        place it ever was.
        <!-- cite: docs/product/positioning.md (an account is the product's core loop); PRIVACY.md#6-security-protections (same backend, RLS-governed) -->
      </p>
      <p>
        If you used the previous browser version in the past: it kept a
        local copy of your data in the browser. That copy was cleared
        automatically the first time you visited the new version, and
        the new one never writes one.
        <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md#10-changes-to-this-privacy-policy; webapp/worker/index.ts (Clear-Site-Data plus a self-unregistering service worker) -->
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
